An iOS window is the screen.

With the whole game shipped -- mod and public, 1.4 GB in the bundle -- the
simulator still shows nothing but magenta, so it was never a missing texture.
The engine renders 1200 frames in four minutes into a surface it reports as
852x393, the screen is 1179x2556, and none of what is drawn is ever composited.

Three things about that window are wrong on iOS, and the GLES probe in this
repository -- which does show its triangle -- does all three differently:

* **Resizable.** An iOS window cannot be resized; it is the screen. Asking for
  one leaves SDL with a layer that does not match.
* **Not fullscreen.** Same reason.
* **No ALLOW_HIGHDPI**, so the drawable is the screen in points rather than
  pixels: 852x393 instead of 2556x1179.

On every other platform the flags are untouched.

--- source/ps/VideoMode.cpp
+++ source/ps/VideoMode.cpp
@@ -396,6 +396,19 @@
 		flags |= SDL_WINDOW_SHOWN | SDL_WINDOW_RESIZABLE;
 		if (m_ConfigEnableHiDPI)
 			flags |= SDL_WINDOW_ALLOW_HIGHDPI;
+#if OS_IOS
+		// An iOS window is the screen: it cannot be resized, and it is always
+		// fullscreen. Asking for a resizable window leaves SDL with a layer
+		// that does not match the screen, and then nothing of what the engine
+		// draws is ever composited -- the simulator shows its placeholder
+		// colour over a game that is rendering happily at five frames a second.
+		//
+		// ALLOW_HIGHDPI on top, because without it the drawable is the screen
+		// in points (852x393 here) rather than pixels, which is the one thing
+		// the working GLES probe in this repository did differently.
+		flags &= ~static_cast<Uint32>(SDL_WINDOW_RESIZABLE);
+		flags |= SDL_WINDOW_FULLSCREEN | SDL_WINDOW_ALLOW_HIGHDPI;
+#endif
 		if (isGLBackend)
 			flags |= SDL_WINDOW_OPENGL;
 		else if (m_Backend == Renderer::Backend::Backend::VULKAN)
